  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how thermal nonlinearity and structural design can be engineered across multiple length scales, from atomic or nanoscale to macroscale, to improve heat‑related energy conversion. The top banner suggests a progression toward “multiscale thermal nonlinearity and structure topology,” while the bottom highlights a balance between maximizing energy efficiency and minimizing carbon footprint through synergy across scales.\n\nAcross the panels, the image groups strategies into nanoscale effects such as nonlinear phononics, resonant vibration, and staggered layers, where atomic motion, lattice thermal conductivity, and polarization appear to be tuned. At the microscale, patterning, metasurfaces, and dimensionality are shown as ways to shape temperature gradients and enhance pyroelectric response. At the mesoscale, electric poling, multilayer capacitor architectures, and pore tailoring seem to increase polarization or pyroelectric performance by reorganizing domains and internal structure. At the macroscale, transverse heat rippling, thermal concentration, and force–heat coupling illustrate device‑level methods for directing heat flow, localizing thermal gradients, and converting mechanical motion into thermal or electrical effects.\n\nOverall, the figure conveys that combining material nonlinearities with tailored topology at several scales can strengthen thermal energy harvesting and management.",
